 \reply
 We have renamed ``Semantic Correctness'' to ``Execution Validity'' throughout the paper, including the abstract, the introduction, and Table~V. The headline improvements (e.g., 130\% over \sedar{} and 100\%--450\% over the baselines) are therefore reported as gains in execution validity rather than semantic correctness. We have also clarified in Section~IV (Evaluation) that execution validity measures executability on the target DBMS and does not imply result equivalence, and we have added a sampled result and state equivalence study. Details can be found in \questionref{1.1}.
 
-% Source: Reviewer C, Weakness (unnumbered).
-\reviewcomment{3.2} RQ2 baselines are somewhat hard to believe: zero bugs in 24 hours for SQUIRREL, GRIFFIN, and SQLancer across all four DBMSs. The baseline configuration is undocumented, several cells are ``-'', for unsupported targets, and the ``528\% over SQLancer'' headline is computed over only the two DBMSs that SQLancer supports.
-
-\reply
-We have documented the configuration of the RQ2 baselines in Section~IV (Evaluation). For \textsc{SQLancer}, \textsc{Squirrel}, and \griffin{}, we use the default configurations, seed corpora, and command lines from their official repositories, and we have included them in our artifact. The zero-bug results reflect that previously reported bugs were fixed in the newer DBMS versions, with no new bugs found. In addition, the 528\% improvement over \textsc{SQLancer} is computed only on DuckDB and ClickHouse, the two target DBMSs it supports, as noted in the footnote of Table~III.
-
